From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan_memory_allocator: ask the driver, not the texel count

An image over 2^28 texels was never offered to VMA: it went straight to the unbacked sparse
fallback. Texel count is not memory size. Ocarina of Time's sun shadow map is 18432x18432 D16 -
339,738,624 texels, past the filter, but only 648 MiB, which an 8 GiB device can hold. With nothing
bound, every write to it is lost and the scene comes out wrong.

Take the later upstream policy (Lichii 75afd1a306, and Eglantine #570 for the byte accounting): probe
the image's real VkMemoryRequirements and leave it unbacked only when the driver cannot report them
or they exceed a quarter of device-local memory. The quarter still keeps the multi-gigabyte sparse
arrays (title 1's 8192x8192, 68 layers, ~23 GB) where they belong.

EDEN_NX2_ALLOC_BYTES=0 restores the texel-only filter, so the two policies can be compared in one
build. The include of <cstdlib> is for std::getenv, which this file did not use before.

What this can break: a large image the old filter rejected now takes real memory. If the probe or the
threshold is wrong, VMA pressure rises, later images fall to host memory or become unbacked, and the
console can show black textures or lose the device. That is the A/B EDEN_NX2_ALLOC_BYTES exists for.

Checked: applies to the tree the 0410-0414 stack produces. Not run on a console.

diff --git a/src/video_core/vulkan_common/vulkan_memory_allocator.cpp b/src/video_core/vulkan_common/vulkan_memory_allocator.cpp
--- a/src/video_core/vulkan_common/vulkan_memory_allocator.cpp
+++ b/src/video_core/vulkan_common/vulkan_memory_allocator.cpp
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@
 #include <algorithm>
 #include <array>
 #include <bit>
+#include <cstdlib>
 #include <limits>
 #include <optional>
 #include <type_traits>
@@ -231,7 +232,30 @@
     // backed: VMA would quietly place them in system memory once VRAM is full. They take the
     // unbacked sparse fallback below.
     const u64 texels = u64{ci.extent.width} * ci.extent.height * ci.extent.depth * ci.arrayLayers;
-    const bool huge = texels > (u64{1} << 28);
+    // The texel count is only a filter: what the image needs depends on its format, levels, layers
+    // and alignment. Ocarina of Time's sun shadow map is 18432x18432 D16 - 339M texels, past the
+    // filter, but only 648 MiB. Calling it unbackable gave it nothing bound, so everything written
+    // to it was lost and the scene came out wrong (Lichii 75afd1a306, Eglantine #570). Ask the
+    // driver what the image would really take instead.
+    // EDEN_NX2_ALLOC_BYTES=0 restores the texel-only filter, for an A/B against the driver probe.
+    static const bool bytes_policy = [] {
+        const char* const value = std::getenv("EDEN_NX2_ALLOC_BYTES");
+        return value == nullptr || value[0] != '0';
+    }();
+    bool huge = !bytes_policy && texels > (u64{1} << 28);
+    if (bytes_policy && texels > (u64{1} << 28)) {
+        VkMemoryRequirements requirements{};
+        VkImage probe = VK_NULL_HANDLE;
+        if (device.GetDispatchLoader().vkCreateImage(*device.GetLogical(), &ci, nullptr, &probe) ==
+            VK_SUCCESS) {
+            device.GetDispatchLoader().vkGetImageMemoryRequirements(*device.GetLogical(), probe,
+                                                                   &requirements);
+            device.GetDispatchLoader().vkDestroyImage(*device.GetLogical(), probe, nullptr);
+        }
+        // A quarter of device-local memory still leaves the multi-gigabyte sparse arrays (title 1's
+        // 8192x8192x68 layers, ~23 GB) on the unbacked path they need.
+        huge = requirements.size == 0 || requirements.size > device.GetDeviceLocalMemory() / 4;
+    }
     VkResult result = huge ? VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY
                            : vmaCreateImage(allocator, &ci, &alloc_ci, &handle, &allocation, &alloc_info);
     if (result == VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY && !huge) {
